\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.5}RQ\ref {rq:security}: how secure custom could have been}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The frontier of RQ\ref {rq:security}. Final Leasehold share over the sweep of the terms-reopening hazard $\varpi $, the arbitrary-fine rate $\varphi $ and institutional resistance $\theta $, with the contour at which the transition fails to complete within the run. The reported result is a bound on how secure customary tenure could have been while England still arrived, which is the question Whittle's and Bailey's evidence raises and cannot settle.}}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq4}{{8}{29}{}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.6}RQ\ref {rq:dispossession}: necessity of dispossession}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Conversion routed through succession vacancies and fine escalation alone, with the crisis channel damped ($\eta _1=\eta _2=0$, eviction suspended), against the full baseline. Leasehold dominance, concentration and a wage-labour pool surviving the damped arm would make dispossession sufficient but not necessary, reconciling Brenner's mechanism with Whittle's Norfolk evidence.}}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq5}{{9}{29}{}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.7}RQ\ref {rq:ecology}: ecology or class structure}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The ALC-derived regional fertility structure flattened to its national mean (\texttt  {uniform\_fertility}), retaining parcel-level jitter, against the baseline. Because the distance regressor still measures from the same county, a distance lag surviving this ablation is not an ecological one; a lag that disappears with it is Moore's point established using Brenner's own mechanisms.}}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq6}{{10}{29}{}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.8}Claims about the model}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The diagnostics of \S  \ref {sec:rq-model}: aggregate tenure composition, the attribution of the farm-size Gini to engrossment rather than turnover, the wage and rent series against each other, and the population accounting -- the share of parcels no household can take up, and the cross-seed correlation between tenure outcomes and population. A failure in any panel is a defect to repair before the results above can be read.}}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:checks}{{11}{29}{}{figure.11}{}}
